        "effective_step": 1,
        "end_token_index": 106,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 107,
        "step_number": 1,
        "text": "Step 1: Let $\\bold{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix}$ be an arbitrary two-dimensional vector. We need to find the smallest positive real number $C$ such that\n\\[\n\\left\\| \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} \\right\\| \\le C \\|\\bold{v}\\|.\n\\]"
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 470,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 471,
        "char_start": 292,
        "effective_step": 2,
        "end_token_index": 182,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 183,
        "step_number": 2,
        "text": "Step 2: Compute the matrix-vector product:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2x + 3y \\\\ -2y \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]"
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 686,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 687,
        "char_start": 472,
        "effective_step": 3,
        "end_token_index": 295,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 296,
        "step_number": 3,
        "text": "Step 3: Calculate the norm of the resulting vector:\n\\[\n\\left\\| \\begin{pmatrix} 2x + 3y \\\\ -2y \\end{pmatrix} \\right\\| = \\sqrt{(2x + 3y)^2 + (-2y)^2} = \\sqrt{4x^2 + 12xy + 9y^2 + 4y^2} = \\sqrt{4x^2 + 12xy + 13y^2}.\n\\]"
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 794,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 795,
        "char_start": 688,
        "effective_step": 4,
        "end_token_index": 344,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 345,
        "step_number": 4,
        "text": "Step 4: Express the inequality we need to satisfy:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{4x^2 + 12xy + 13y^2} \\le C \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}.\n\\]"
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 894,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 895,
        "char_start": 796,
        "effective_step": 5,
        "end_token_index": 391,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 392,
        "step_number": 5,
        "text": "Step 5: Square both sides to remove the square root:\n\\[\n4x^2 + 12xy + 13y^2 \\le C^2 (x^2 + y^2).\n\\]"
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 1238,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 1239,
        "char_start": 896,
        "effective_step": 6,
        "end_token_index": 505,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 506,
        "step_number": 6,
        "text": "Step 6: To find the smallest possible value of $C$, consider the worst-case scenario where the left side is maximized relative to the right side. This occurs when the terms involving $x$ and $y$ are balanced. We can use the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality to find the maximum ratio:\n\\[\n4x^2 + 12xy + 13y^2 \\le (4 + 13)(x^2 + y^2) = 17(x^2 + y^2).\n\\]"
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 1344,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 1345,
        "char_start": 1240,
        "effective_step": 7,
        "end_token_index": 540,
        "near_end": true,
        "prefix_token_count": 541,
        "step_number": 7,
        "text": "Step 7: Therefore, the smallest $C$ that satisfies the inequality for all $x$ and $y$ is $C = \\sqrt{17}$."
      }
    ]
