  "raw_text": "Step 1: Let $\\bold{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix}$ be an arbitrary two-dimensional vector. We need to find the smallest positive real number $C$ such that\n\\[\n\\left\\| \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} \\right\\| \\le C \\|\\bold{v}\\|.\n\\]\nStep 2: Compute the matrix-vector product:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2x + 3y \\\\ -2y \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nStep 3: Calculate the norm of the resulting vector:\n\\[\n\\left\\| \\begin{pmatrix} 2x + 3y \\\\ -2y \\end{pmatrix} \\right\\| = \\sqrt{(2x + 3y)^2 + (-2y)^2} = \\sqrt{4x^2 + 12xy + 9y^2 + 4y^2} = \\sqrt{4x^2 + 12xy + 13y^2}.\n\\]\nStep 4: Express the inequality we need to satisfy:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{4x^2 + 12xy + 13y^2} \\le C \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}.\n\\]\nStep 5: Square both sides to remove the square root:\n\\[\n4x^2 + 12xy + 13y^2 \\le C^2 (x^2 + y^2).\n\\]\nStep 6: To find the smallest possible value of $C$, consider the worst-case scenario where the left side is maximized relative to the right side. This occurs when the terms involving $x$ and $y$ are balanced. We can use the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality to find the maximum ratio:\n\\[\n4x^2 + 12xy + 13y^2 \\le (4 + 13)(x^2 + y^2) = 17(x^2 + y^2).\n\\]\nStep 7: Therefore, the smallest $C$ that satisfies the inequality for all $x$ and $y$ is $C = \\sqrt{17}$.\n\nFinal answer: $\\boxed{\\sqrt{17}}$<|im_end|>",
